window.docContents[227].resolve({"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Compactness-and-the-direct-method":{"contents":"Formalized: relative weak compactness for bounded Hilbert-space families, weakly\nclosed sets cut out by arbitrary families of continuous linear constraints,\npreservation of those constraints under weak limits, and their pointwise\nlifting to almost-everywhere constraints on Bochner  trajectories.\nThe lifted constraint spaces are weakly closed, survive weak  limits,\nand preserve weak compactness when imposed on bounded weakly closed families.\nContinuous linear functionals that vanish on the kernel of a closed-range\noperator factor continuously through its range and have a unique\nrange-valued Hilbert multiplier; this is the abstract pressure-recovery step\nfor a divergence operator.\nComponentwise weak convergence in two Hilbert spaces also preserves every\ncommon bound on a nonnegative weighted sum of squared norms, which is the\nkinetic-plus-dissipation limit passage used in energy-class constructions.\nThe formalized layer also includes weak lower semicontinuity of convex\nfunctionals, coercive direct-method theorems, Sobolev integral applications,\nspatial Rellich–Kondrachov compactness, and the weak Bochner compactness and\nLions–Magenes machinery used by the Galerkin chapters.\nFor compact dense Gelfand inclusions of real Hilbert spaces on a fixed\nfinite interval , the abstract Aubin–Lions theorem is formalized for\nLions–Magenes trajectories: uniform  bounds and\n bounds on their weak time derivatives imply relative\ncompactness in .\nFor Hölder-related exponents , strong  and\n convergence passes continuous bilinear pointwise operations to strong\n convergence. In particular, quadratic strong  convergence yields strong\n convergence. Weak dual trajectories can also be paired with strongly\nconvergent states inside the time integral. Continuous trilinear forms have\nabstract skew-symmetry lemmas for cubic energy cancellation and quadratic\ndifference estimates, a bounded-set Lipschitz estimate for the induced\ndual-valued quadratic map, and a strong–strong–weak limit theorem for\nbounded weak test families. Quadratic Gelfand weak equations satisfy both\na state-tested energy identity and an exact difference equation. At\nthe Galerkin level, skew cancellation transfers the dimension-independent\npivot and integrated coercive estimates from the linear equation to the\nquadratic equation. Finite-partition ODE solutions can be\ncontinued inside a time-dependent family of invariant sets, including\nmoving energy envelopes. The quadratic mass-inverted Galerkin vector field\nhas explicit growth and bounded-set Lipschitz estimates. Combining its local\nPicard theorem, finite-dimensional pivot norm equivalence, coercivity, and\nskew cancellation gives global Galerkin existence on every finite interval.\nThe argument uses no Navier–Stokes structure. A sign-unrestricted differential Grönwall\ntheorem and its dissipative corollary provide a complementary scalar route:\nif , , and , then a\ndifferentiable trajectory satisfying  on\n satisfies  throughout that interval.\n\nPartial: Aubin–Lions compactness covers Hilbert Gelfand triples with\n time integrability of both the trajectories and their weak time\nderivatives. Around it the evolution layer has coordinated common-filter weak\nlimits, identification of weak time derivatives, and the basic nonlinear limit\npassages that use its strong convergence.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Foundations\u0009Coverage and Open Problems\u0009Foundational Coverage and Open Problems","header":"3.5.1.5. Compactness and the direct method","id":"/Foundations/Coverage-and-Open-Problems/#PDEAtlas--Foundations--Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Foundational-Coverage-and-Open-Problems--Compactness-and-the-direct-method"},"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility--Compatibility-and-Differentiated-Galerkin-Chains":{"contents":"Differentiating the wave equation in time creates a finite hierarchy of wave\nproblems. Compatibility conditions supply the initial data for every level,\nand one common compactness extraction is needed so that adjacent weak limits\nremain derivatives of each other.\n\nThe data consist of a finite Sobolev time jet, initial displacement and\nvelocity of matching spatial orders, and the formal initial derivativesCompatibility requires each  to lie in the spatial space prescribed for\n, to have the homogeneous Dirichlet trace when required,\nand to satisfy this recurrence. Compatibility thus fixes both the regularity\nbudget and the boundary values of the formal derivatives.\n\nAt order , consider  with\n and . Evaluating the equation at time zero gives the\nfirst nontrivial compatibility value\n\n\n\nHigher regularity requires this expression to lie in the space prescribed for\nthe second time derivative and to satisfy the corresponding boundary\ncompatibility. The recursive formal data encode this same calculation at\nevery later order.\n\nA Sobolev time jet on a nontrivial interval admits coherent dual-valued\nrepresentatives , all but the last absolutely continuous, such thatEach representative agrees almost everywhere with the Dirichlet load of the matching jet\nentry, so later Galerkin systems use one compatible chain rather than\nunrelated representatives of the weak derivatives.\n\nStart with a representative of the highest weak time derivative. Descend\nthrough the jet, choosing at each level the Sobolev representative whose weak\nderivative is the representative already fixed one level above. The Sobolev\ntime-derivative identities hold almost everywhere, and the canonical\nembeddings into the dual energy space preserve both those identities and the\nprescribed Bochner integrability.\n\nThe nonterminal forcing representatives have canonical initial traces\n for . These are the values used in the compatibility\nrecurrence. The highest forcing derivative needs only its Bochner\nintegrability.\n\nCompatible wave data determine the initial displacement  and velocity\n of each differentiated problem, for . Adjacent\nlevels satisfy the formal acceleration relationThe equality is interpreted in the dual energy space, using the pivot\nembedding for . It is a compatibility identity for the initial data.\n\nEmbed each formal compatibility value  into the common ambient energy\nspace and pair adjacent values as displacement and velocity. The\ncompatibility recurrence\n rearranges to the required initial acceleration\nequation. Coherence of the forcing representatives ensures that all levels\nuse the same time-zero data rather than independent choices.\n\nEach differentiated problem could be solved by the Galerkin method for weak\nwaves, but separate compactness extractions would produce limits with no\nrelation to one another.\n\nCompatible initial jets, stable dense trial spaces, Ritz orthogonality, coherent forcing,\nsymmetric coercivity, and uniform forcing budgets produce weak-wave limits\n along one common filter, with\n\nFinite-dimensional differentiated trajectories are built first. Uniform\nenergy bounds provide bounded families at every level. One common compactness\nextraction passes all equations and adjacent derivative identities to the\nlimit, including the convergent projected initial values.\n\nSymmetry of  enters the chain theorem through the Ritz property of the\nprojected initial data and through the energy identity behind the uniform\nbounds. When  has a nonsymmetric remainder, the energy bounds can\nstill be run on the symmetric coercive part , but Ritz orthogonality must\nbe imposed for the full operator.\n\nLet the autonomous operator split as , with  symmetric and\ncoercive and with a possibly nonsymmetric remainder satisfyingIn addition to compatible data, stable dense trial projections, and a uniform\nforcing budget, assume full-operator Ritz orthogonality:for each initial jet entry  used in the adjacent compatibility recurrence.\nHere  is the restriction of the stable pivot projection to .\nThis condition is separate from stability and density, and orthogonality for\n alone does not imply it. Under these hypotheses, the theorem constructs\na common finite chain of weak-wave limits for , retaining every adjacent\ntime-derivative identity.\n\nFull-operator Ritz orthogonality makes the projected initial jet satisfy the\ndifferentiated finite-dimensional equations. The uniform energy bounds take\n as the coercive symmetric energy form.\nThe mixed estimate on  absorbs the remainder in that energy estimate,\nat the cost of a larger exponential rate. Apply the common finite-chain compactness extraction\nto all differentiated Galerkin trajectories at once; weak convergence passes\nboth the  and  terms to the limit and preserves every adjacent\ntime-derivative identity.\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas\u0009Hyperbolic Equations\u0009Weak Waves and Evolution\u0009Higher Regularity and Compatibility","header":"7.3.3.1. Compatibility and Differentiated Galerkin Chains","id":"/Hyperbolic-Equations/Weak-Waves-and-Evolution/#PDEAtlas--Hyperbolic-Equations--Weak-Waves-and-Evolution--Higher-Regularity-and-Compatibility--Compatibility-and-Differentiated-Galerkin-Chains"},"/Related-Work/#related-work":{"contents":"Computing a PDE residual, bounding an approximation error, and constructing a\nsolution are different mathematical tasks. A symbolic identity can verify a\ncandidate formula; a numerical enclosure can certify an estimate; an existence\nproof must supply the analytic argument connecting its hypotheses to a solution.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"PDEAtlas","header":"2. Related Work","id":"/Related-Work/#related-work"}});